<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ardahan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ardahan is a city in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, the capital of Ardahan Province, on the upper Kura river near the Georgian border. Long held by Georgian rulers and then by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, it was taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1877–78. It returned to Ottoman control in 1918, was contested by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, and became part of Turkey under the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a> in 1921.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ardahan.jpg" alt="Ardahan" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jaba1977 · Attribution · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ardahan.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ardahan lies in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, on the upper course of the Kura river and south of the Yalnızçam Mountains. The climate is humid continental, with brief, mild summers and very cold winters. Snow cover lasts from late October to mid April. In the 1640s the Ottoman traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evliya-celebi">Evliya Çelebi</a> described the fortress as sitting on an inaccessible cliff, with no gardens or orchards because of the cold.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ardahan lay in the region of Gogarene, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> calls part of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> taken from the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a transit point for goods from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> region. From the 8th to 10th centuries Bagrationi princes of Tao-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klarjeti">Klarjeti</a> held it as Artaani, and it was part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> from the 11th to the 15th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> took it in the 1230s, and the Georgian princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Samtskhe</a> recaptured it in 1266. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-amasya">Peace of Amasya</a> in 1555 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> annexed the western part of Samtskhe, and Ardahan became a formidable fortress-town.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> took the town after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1877 to 1878. Its people were mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>, Pontic Greeks, Caucasus Jews, Russians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> and others, and it prospered, exporting fruit, smoked lamb, wheat and wood. On 25 December 1914 the Ottoman army occupied it and massacred many Armenians, Pontic Greeks and Georgians; the Russians retook it on 3 January 1915. The Ottoman army regained it on 6 March 1918, the Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> occupied it on 20 April 1919, and Turkish Nationalists took it on 23 February 1921, after which the remaining Armenians, Pontic Greeks and Georgians fled. A treaty then confirmed it as Turkish.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ardahan is the seat of Ardahan Province and Ardahan District, a province it became in 1992 by separating from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a>. Its population was 22,927 in 2021, against 7,228 in 1960, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> and Turks lived there. The walls of the fortress, Ardahan Kalesi, are of late antique or medieval origin and were rebuilt several times; in the 19th century they were adapted for small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ardahan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The First Republic of Armenia, declared on 28 May 1918, was invaded in 1920 by Turkish nationalist forces and by the 11th Red Army, which entered Yerevan in early December; the Treaty of Kars then ceded Kars, <b>Ardahan</b> and Surmalu, with Mount Ararat, to Turkey.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Governed from Akhaltsikhe, the eyalet included <b>Ardahan</b>, Artvin and the upper Kura valleys.</blockquote><a href="/state/childir-eyalet">Childir Eyalet</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Armistice of Mudros in October 1918, the Ottoman Empire had to withdraw from Kars, <b>Ardahan</b> and Batum, lands it had gained by the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk.</blockquote><a href="/state/southwestern-caucasian-republic">Southwestern Caucasian Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Facing an Ottoman offensive and the loss of Kars, <b>Ardahan</b> and Batum under the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk, the Transcaucasian Sejm in Tiflis declared independence from Russia on 22 April 1918, replacing the Transcaucasian Commissariat with a federal republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/transcaucasian-democratic-federative-republic">Transcaucasian Democratic Federative Republic</a></li></ul></section>
